{
  "0": "Across all four datasets, switch the existing two bidirectional Transformer blocks from pre-norm to post-norm by setting norm_first=False, retaining the final LayerNorm. Conventional BERT-style residual normalization may improve masked-item representations under full-catalog cross-entropy. Keep embeddings, widths, heads, features, sampling, readout, loss and optimizer fixed. Preserve exclusive-mask training and appended-mask inference; use the same architecture in the predictor writer.",
  "1": "Across all four datasets, retain full-catalog masked-item cross-entropy but weight targets at the final sequence position by 2 and all other masked targets by 1; divide the summed weighted losses by the summed weights. This emphasizes the position used during appended-mask inference while retaining bidirectional cloze supervision elsewhere. Preserve masking and negative sampling, architecture, embeddings, scoring, optimizer and all training settings.",
  "2": "Across all four datasets, jointly reduce learning rate from 1e-3 to 5e-4, increase dropout from 0.2 to 0.3 and set weight decay to 1e-5. Smaller updates coupled with stronger regularization may reduce overfitting of tied embeddings under full-catalog cross-entropy. Retain width 64, two blocks, two heads, history length 128, batch size 128, existing Adam optimizer type, loss and readout. Keep seed 42, the 200-epoch cap and 200 batches per epoch.",
  "3": "Across all four datasets, normalize every nonpadding item embedding to a fixed L2 norm sqrt(2*64/(itemnum+2+64)), matching the expected initial Xavier row norm. Use this effective tied table consistently for context lookup, masked-item cross-entropy and candidate scoring; keep padding zero and the exclusive mask as a separate ID. Removing item-norm variation may improve angular discrimination without increasing width, whose earlier expansion regressed. Preserve positional embeddings, topology, history, loss definition and optimizer; update the predictor writer consistently."
}
